"""Build the B1 report from recorded corner artifacts, without recomputation."""
import html,json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent

def main():
    d=json.loads((ROOT/'measurements.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'));records=d['records']
    rows=[];sections=[]
    for r in records:
        n=r['image'][4:8];s=r['strongest_metrics'];a=r['anms_metrics']
        rows.append(f'<tr><td><a href="#image-{n}">{n}</a></td><td>{r["candidate_count"]:,}</td><td>{r["selected_count"]}</td><td>{s["median_nearest_neighbor_px"]:.2f} → {a["median_nearest_neighbor_px"]:.2f}</td><td>{s["occupied_grid_cells"]} → {a["occupied_grid_cells"]}</td></tr>')
        matrix='\n'.join('  '.join(f'{v: .10g}' for v in row) for row in r['H_original_to_working'])
        sections.append(f'''<section class="report-section image-case" id="image-{n}"><p class="eyebrow">IMG_{n} · {r['original_size'][0]} × {r['original_size'][1]} original pixels</p><h2>{r['candidate_count']:,} candidates.<br>{r['selected_count']} selected.</h2><figure class="result-figure"><a href="figures/IMG_{n}_comparison.jpg"><img src="figures/IMG_{n}_comparison.jpg" alt="IMG {n}: Harris candidates, strongest 500, and ANMS 500 corner overlays" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Same 1200 × 1600 image. Left: all candidates. Center: the {r['selected_count']} strongest responses. Right: the {r['selected_count']} largest ANMS radii. Rings mark corners, not cross-image matches.</figcaption></figure><p class="resource-links"><a href="../A1/images/IMG_{n}.jpg">Original photo</a> · <a href="working/IMG_{n}.png">Working PNG</a> · <a href="figures/IMG_{n}_candidates.jpg">All candidates</a> · <a href="figures/IMG_{n}_strongest.jpg">Strongest 500</a> · <a href="figures/IMG_{n}_anms.jpg">ANMS 500</a> · <a href="{r['points_npz']}">All candidate data (NPZ)</a> · <a href="{r['points_json']}">Selected points (JSON)</a></p><p>Nearest-neighbor median: {s['median_nearest_neighbor_px']:.2f} → {a['median_nearest_neighbor_px']:.2f} working pixels. Occupied 6 × 8 grid cells: {s['occupied_grid_cells']} → {a['occupied_grid_cells']} of 48. {r['unbounded_radius_count']} unbounded radii. Minimum finite selected radius: {r['selected_finite_radius_min']:.2f} pixels, not minimum pairwise spacing.</p><details><summary>Response threshold and original-to-working transform</summary><p>Positive response maximum: {r['response_max']:.9g}. Absolute threshold: {r['absolute_response_threshold']:.9g}. The same relative threshold, scales and border are used for all images.</p><pre>{html.escape(matrix)}</pre><p>Working pixel centers are integer-valued. Original locations may be fractional because of the recorded resize transform.</p></details></section>''')
    example=next(r for r in records if '8446' in r['image'])
    page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>B.1 · Find the Corners</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../A3/page.css"></head><body class="report-page"><main class="shell">
<header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="../index.html">Stitching Photo<br>Mosaics</a><nav class="header-nav" aria-label="B.1 navigation"><a href="../index.html">Project home <span>01</span></a><a href="#method">Harris &amp; ANMS <span>02</span></a><a href="#results">Seven image results <span>03</span></a></nav></header>
<section class="project-hero"><p class="eyebrow">SYDE 671 · Assignment 3 · James Xie</p><h1>B.1 / FIND<br>THE CORNERS.</h1><p class="project-deck">Find strong local structure. Then make room for corners across the image.</p><div class="project-hero-rule"><span>Part B · Corner detection</span><span>7 photographs · 500 ANMS points each</span></div></section>
<section class="report-section"><h2>01 / Strength is only half the story.</h2><figure class="result-figure"><a href="#image-8446"><img src="figures/IMG_8446_comparison.jpg" alt="Harris candidates compared with equal-count strongest and ANMS selections" loading="eager"></a><figcaption>All Harris candidates, strongest 500, and ANMS 500 on IMG_8446. ANMS spreads the selected points across textured image regions while keeping them within the same detected-candidate pool.</figcaption></figure><p>Harris and ANMS process the seven A.4 photos independently, without using their homographies. B.2–B.4 handle descriptors, matching and stitching.</p></section>
<section class="report-section" id="method"><h2>02 / Single-scale Harris response.</h2><p>Lanczos resizing produces 1200 × 1600 working PNGs. Detection uses one scale, integer centers and no orientation estimate. Grayscale is 0.299R + 0.587G + 0.114B on RGB/255.</p><p>After Gaussian smoothing at σ = 1.0, centered differences give gradients. Their products are integrated at σ = 1.5 to form the 2 × 2 second-moment matrix.</p><div class="formula">M = Gaussian₁.₅ * [[Iₓ², IₓIᵧ], [IₓIᵧ, Iᵧ²]]
R = det(M) − 0.04 · trace(M)²</div><p>Flat regions respond weakly; isolated edges respond negatively; corners can respond positively. This is classical Harris. Brown et al. instead use det(M)/trace(M).</p><p>Candidates are positive 3 × 3 maxima above 0.001 of the global maximum, excluding a 24-pixel border for 40 × 40 descriptors. Plateau ties remain candidates; ANMS then ranks the points.</p><p>Harris and ANMS are handwritten. OpenCV supplies Gaussian and local-maximum filtering, not feature detection.</p></section>
<section class="report-section"><h2>03 / A radius set by stronger neighbors.</h2><p>ANMS measures distance to the nearest sufficiently stronger point across the full candidate pool. The robust factor is 0.9, following section 3 of <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2005/06/cvpr05.pdf">Brown, Szeliski &amp; Winder (2005)</a>.</p><div class="formula">rᵢ = minⱼ ||pᵢ − pⱼ||, subject to Rᵢ &lt; 0.9 Rⱼ
keep the 500 candidates with the largest rᵢ</div><p>Without a stronger neighbor, r is infinite. Ties use response, then y and x. NPZ stores infinity; JSON uses null with radius_unbounded. Blocks of 128 limit memory, but cost remains O(N²).</p><p>The baseline selects the same number of strongest candidates. ANMS spreads points across textured regions; its radius concerns stronger neighbors, not a guaranteed minimum pairwise spacing.</p></section>
<section class="report-section" id="results"><h2>04 / Compare the distribution.</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Image</th><th>Candidates</th><th>Points per method</th><th>Median nearest distance (px)<br>Strongest → ANMS</th><th>Occupied grid cells / 48<br>Strongest → ANMS</th></tr></thead><tbody>''' + ''.join(rows) + '''</tbody></table></div><p>Distances use working pixels. Occupancy counts populated cells in a 6 × 8 grid. Equal-sized selections describe spatial distribution, not matching or mosaic accuracy.</p><p>ANMS increases spacing and coverage in all seven images. Moving vegetation and reflections can still yield poor matches, and single-scale detection is not scale invariant. B.3 and B.4 evaluate matching and geometry.</p></section>''' + ''.join(sections) + '''
<section class="report-section"><h2>05 / Coordinates ready for the next stage.</h2><div class="formula">original_xy = (working_xy + 0.5) · original_size / working_size − 0.5</div><p>Coordinates are (x, y) pixel centers, rightward and downward. NPZ saves all candidates, scores, radii, both selections and transforms. JSON saves the selected 500 points; B.2 reuses the same working PNG.</p><p>Originals and Part A data are unchanged. Parameters, versions, hashes and metrics are saved in <a href="measurements.json">measurements.json</a>.</p></section>
<section class="report-section"><h2>Reproduce &amp; inspect.</h2><pre>python run_corners.py
python build_report.py
python -m unittest test_corners -v</pre><p>Run from B1 using the existing Python environment with NumPy, Pillow and OpenCV. No dependency was added.</p><p><a href="corners.py">Harris, candidate filtering &amp; handwritten ANMS</a> · <a href="run_corners.py">Real-image experiment</a> · <a href="test_corners.py">Six analytical unit checks</a> · <a href="measurements.json">Parameters &amp; results</a></p><p>Checks cover synthetic corners, edges, known radii, ties, invalid inputs and coordinate conversion. Real data checks verify 20 radii per image, selections and input hashes.</p><p class="method-note">B.2–B.4 show descriptors, matches and the automatic mosaic, which uses seven images against the clarified minimum of three.</p></section>
<nav class="project-home-nav" aria-label="Project navigation"><a class="solid-link" href="../index.html"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span>Back to Project Home</a></nav>
<footer class="site-footer"><span>SYDE 671 · Assignment 3 · James Xie</span><span>B.1 · Harris corners &amp; ANMS</span></footer></main></body></html>'''
    (ROOT/'report.html').write_text(page,encoding='utf-8')
    (ROOT/'README.md').write_text('''# B.1 - Harris corners and ANMS

Open report.html for all7 images, each with full candidate, strongest500 and ANMS500 overlays. Original input photos and Part A numerical artifacts are unchanged.

corners.py computes the classical single-scale Harris response, positive3x3 local maxima and exact blocked ANMS with robust0.9. Working images are1200x1600 lossless PNGs, resized from original RGB by Lanczos. Response threshold0.001*max, border24, derivative sigma1, integration sigma1.5, k0.04. No built-in corner detector or matching API.

points/*.npz stores every candidate's (x,y) working/original coordinates, response, suppression radius (inf when no sufficiently stronger neighbor), both500-point selections and coordinate transform. points/*.json stores selected ANMS points, with null+radius_unbounded for infinite radii. working/*.png preserves exact source samples for B2.

Run from B1:
```powershell
python run_corners.py
python build_report.py
python -m unittest test_corners -v
```

Six analytical unit checks passed. Real artifacts verify20 sampled radii per image, selected-index validity, coordinate transforms and unchanged input hashes. Spatial metrics are descriptive, not matching or mosaic accuracy. Single scale and integer feature centers only; no descriptors, matching, RANSAC or automatic mosaic. No new dependencies.

ANMS source: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2005/06/cvpr05.pdf section3. The paper uses a harmonic-mean corner response; this implementation uses classical Harris det-ktrace², an explicitly documented choice.
''',encoding='utf-8')
    print('B1 report and README generated from seven saved datasets.')

if __name__=='__main__':main()
